	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>How they differ</h2>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Proven components vs a clean rewrite</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu doesn't implement mail protocols itself: it wraps Postfix, Dovecot and Rspamd,
			components with long production histories, in small purpose-built containers behind
			an nginx front end, with Redis for state. Stalwart implements SMTP, IMAP, POP3, JMAP
			and the DAV protocols itself, in memory-safe Rust, as a single binary with a published
			security audit. That gives a coherent design and very modest resource use; the cost is
			a younger codebase that is still pre-1.0, with its roadmap's next milestone being to
			finalise the database schema.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Setup and footprint</h3>
		<p>
			Both are light by mail-server standards. Mailu's documented minimum is 1 GB of RAM plus
			1 GB of swap without antivirus, or 3 GB with ClamAV enabled. You configure it through
			a web setup wizard that generates a <code>docker-compose.yml</code> and a
			<code>mailu.env</code>, choosing optional pieces such as antivirus, webmail, CalDAV and
			full-text search. Stalwart idles at around 100 MB and documents 1 GB as comfortable for
			5 to 10 users. It installs as a single binary on Linux, macOS, FreeBSD or Windows, or
			as a Docker container, and is configured from its web admin.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Protocols, webmail and groupware</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart is the more protocol-complete server: JMAP for mail, calendars and contacts,
			plus CalDAV, CardDAV and WebDAV, all built in. Mailu covers SMTP, IMAP and POP3, and
			adds calendar and contacts through an optional Radicale container.
		</p>
		<p>
			In the browser the balance flips. Mailu bundles a webmail, Roundcube or SnappyMail, so
			users can read mail on day one. Stalwart's web interface is for administration and
			account self-service, not reading mail, so you add a webmail yourself or rely on
			desktop and mobile clients. Neither speaks Exchange ActiveSync.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Filtering</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu ships Rspamd with autolearn, greylisting and DMARC/SPF checks, plus optional ClamAV
			and oletools for malicious-attachment scanning. Stalwart has its own built-in spam
			filter with a statistical classifier, Pyzor and spam traps (LLM analysis is an
			Enterprise feature); antivirus means running ClamAV yourself and connecting it as a
			milter.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Scale, identity and APIs</h3>
		<p>
			Stalwart is built to scale out: native clustering, Kubernetes or Swarm, pluggable
			storage (RocksDB, PostgreSQL, MySQL, SQLite, FoundationDB, S3-compatible, Azure), and
			LDAP, OIDC or SQL directories. Read replicas, sharded storage, SCIM and multi-tenancy
			are Enterprise features. Mailu is a single-host Compose stack; a community Helm chart
			exists for Kubernetes, though its own docs say it is looking for maintainers. Both have
			an admin API: Mailu's REST API covers everything its admin UI does, with a built-in
			Swagger UI.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Upgrades</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu upgrades by checking the changelog for compose and environment changes, then
			pulling new images and recreating the containers. Stalwart's documentation says there
			is no automated upgrade tool yet: patch releases are safe, but minor releases may require
			configuration changes or data migrations, so it recommends reading the release notes
			first and upgrading only when necessary. Neither takes a pre-update snapshot, runs a
			health-check gate or rolls back on failure.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Licensing and cost</h3>
		<p>
			Mailu is MIT-licensed throughout, with no paid edition. Stalwart is dual-licensed: the
			AGPL-3.0 core is free, and a proprietary Enterprise edition, priced per mailbox, adds
			multi-tenancy, SCIM provisioning, LLM spam filtering, mail undelete, read replicas and
			sharded storage, live telemetry and priority support.
		</p>

		<h3 class="alt-h3">Programmatic sending</h3>
		<p>
			Neither is a transactional sending platform. Mailu's API manages configuration, and
			applications send through SMTP submission as a real mailbox. Stalwart's JMAP can submit
			mail and its webhooks report events, but your application integrates against a mail
			protocol. There are no domain-scoped sending keys, per-domain rate limits or delivery
			analytics in either.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
