					<li>You want the proven Postfix, Dovecot and Rspamd core in a small Docker stack.</li>
					<li>Your users need webmail on day one.</li>
					<li>You want an MIT-licensed stack configured through a web wizard.</li>
					<li>You're running a single host and don't need clustering.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
			<div class="feature-card">
				<h3>Choose Stalwart if</h3>
				<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
					<li>You want JMAP, or CalDAV/CardDAV/WebDAV built into the server itself.</li>
					<li>You need clustering, LDAP/OIDC directories or a specific storage backend.</li>
					<li>You want to run outside Docker, or on macOS, FreeBSD or Windows.</li>
					<li>You're comfortable with pre-1.0 software and reading release notes before each upgrade.</li>
				</ul>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>The third option: Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>
			If what's missing from <em>both</em> is a transactional sending API, or an update you
			don't have to supervise, that's the gap Vectis Mail is built to close. It runs the same
			proven Postfix and Dovecot core as Mailu, with Rspamd by default, installed by one
			guided command:
		</p>
		<div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
				<li>Runs on Ubuntu 24.04 LTS from 2 GB of RAM without ClamAV (4 GB recommended), in 10 containers by default.</li>
				<li>Domains, mailboxes, aliases and API keys managed from an admin dashboard or the REST API, taking effect immediately with no restarts.</li>
				<li>A 40+ endpoint REST API: transactional sending (single and batch, with attachments), HMAC-signed inbound webhooks, domain-scoped API keys with per-domain rate limits, and analytics.</li>
				<li>Atomic 6-phase updates (snapshot, migrate, pull, deploy, health-check, complete) with automatic rollback, and <code>vectis verify</code> to prove the box runs exactly what was published.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
		<p style="margin-top: 1rem;">
			To be equally clear about where Vectis Mail doesn't fit: it's Docker-only and
			single-node, and it doesn't ship JMAP, calendar, contacts or ActiveSync today. If
			groupware or a multi-node cluster is the core requirement, it isn't your answer. See
			the dedicated <a href="/alternatives/stalwart/">Vectis Mail vs Stalwart</a> comparison,
			and the <a href="/guides/best-self-hosted-email-servers-2026/">best self-hosted email
			servers guide</a> for where Mailu fits in the wider landscape.
		</p>
		<div class="hero-buttons" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/" class="btn-primary">Install Vectis Mail free</a>
			<a href="/pricing/" class="btn-secondary">View pricing</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Mailu or Stalwart better?</h3>
				<p>
					They suit different priorities. Mailu packages proven components (Postfix,
					Dovecot, Rspamd) as a lightweight Docker Compose stack with webmail, an admin UI
					and a REST API, configured through a web setup wizard and MIT-licensed. Stalwart
					reimplements every protocol in one Rust binary, adding JMAP, built-in
					CalDAV/CardDAV/WebDAV and native clustering, but it is still pre-1.0 and ships
					no webmail. Choose Mailu for a small, conventional, proven stack; choose
					Stalwart for JMAP, built-in groupware or multi-node scale.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How much RAM do Mailu and Stalwart need?</h3>
				<p>
					Both are light. Mailu's documented minimum is 1 GB of RAM plus 1 GB of swap
					without antivirus, or 3 GB plus 1 GB of swap with ClamAV. Stalwart idles at
					roughly 100 MB, and its documentation says 1 GB is generally sufficient for 5 to
					10 users; antivirus means running ClamAV separately as a milter, which needs its
					own memory.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Do Mailu and Stalwart support calendar and contacts?</h3>
				<p>
					Both can. Stalwart has CalDAV, CardDAV and WebDAV built in, plus JMAP for
					calendars and contacts. Mailu offers calendar and contacts through an optional
					Radicale container you enable at setup. Neither supports Exchange ActiveSync.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is Stalwart production-ready?</h3>
				<p>
					Stalwart describes itself as feature complete, but it is still pre-1.0
					(v0.16.25 as of October 2026). Its own upgrade documentation says there is no
					automated upgrade tool yet: minor releases may require configuration changes or
					data migrations, so it recommends reading the release notes and upgrading only
					when necessary.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does either have a transactional sending API?</h3>
				<p>
					Not in the SendGrid sense. Mailu's REST API manages configuration (domains,
					users, aliases), and applications send through SMTP submission as a real
					mailbox. Stalwart offers JMAP, which can submit mail, plus event webhooks.
					Neither offers domain-scoped sending keys, per-domain rate limits or delivery
					analytics. Vectis Mail is a self-hosted platform built to add that sending API
					alongside mailbox hosting.
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

